<WorkBlock id="context">

## Operating context

Quality Counts had an experimental web app for exploring traffic-count data, but this was its first attempt at production software. The company asked me to lead the creative direction, redesign DataPoint, and help establish a software practice with its internal team and a contracted development team.

City planners and traffic engineers needed enough detail to analyze intersections and midblocks. Residents needed a faster way to check traffic conditions and test assumptions about their communities. The same product had to support both groups without turning every view into an expert-only interface.

The original product centered on a full-screen map. Customers selected an intersection or midblock, reviewed an overview panel, and opened a separate view for detailed count data. It exposed useful information, but it did not provide a clear structure for search, comparison, deeper analysis, or future modules.

### Constraints

- I entered the project without prior traffic-planning or city-government experience.
- Quality Counts was building its first production software product.
- Historical traffic data varied in structure and quality.
- Residents and technical customers brought very different domain knowledge.
- Each customer had a separate DataPoint instance and city-specific URL.

</WorkBlock>

